From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] d3d11: video decoders on Vulkan Video (H.264)

Programs that decode video themselves through Direct3D 11 -- Chromium and
Electron programs, Firefox, VLC, mpv, Kodi -- ask the device for a video
decoder (ID3D11VideoDevice::CreateVideoDecoder) and hand it DXVA buffers.
DXVK had none (E_NOTIMPL, "Stub"), so every such program decoded on the CPU.

The device now takes a video decode queue (VK_KHR_video_queue,
video_decode_queue, video_decode_h264) when it has one. A D3D11 video
decoder is a Vulkan video session: the DXVA picture parameters, inverse
quantization matrices and slice control (public DXVA H.264 specification)
are turned into the Vulkan Video standard's parameter sets, picture and
reference information; the bitstream (short slice format, whole NAL units)
is decoded into a DPB of the decoder's own, and the picture is copied into
the program's NV12 output texture on DXVK's queue after the decode.

The profile offered is H.264 VLD without film grain, 8-bit 4:2:0,
progressive; interlaced pictures are refused (E_NOTIMPL). Written from the
DXVA and Vulkan specifications.

Offered only where proven: d3d11.videoDecode (dxvk.conf) is Auto by
default, which offers decoders on NVIDIA's proprietary driver only (bit-exact
against software decode there); True offers them on any driver with H.264
decode, for testing; False never.

Notes: the session's largest picture must be the device's (NVIDIA's driver
refused every picture of a session made for the stream's own size with
VK_ERROR_INVALID_VIDEO_STD_PARAMETERS_KHR at vkEndCommandBuffer); slices
name their picture parameter set, so its id is read from the first slice
header.
